              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em inner product
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (also “scalar product” in the sense of “with values in the
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalars"} scalars
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mtext -
                  %mo ,
                  %mtext -
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\text{-},\text{-}\rangle
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalars"} scalars
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v_1,\, v_2
                  \\,\in\,
                  V
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\vdash
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\langle v_1,\, v_2 \rangle
                  \\,\in\,
                  \\mathbb{K}
          %p
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+form"} bilinear
            (or rather
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sesquilinear+form"} sesquilinear
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            is understood with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            with
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
          %p
            Often one requires such a pairing to be non-degenerate or even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive-definite"} positive-definite
            in order to qualify as an inner product. Conventions depend on context.
          %p
            For example, the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+inner+product"}> Hermitian
            ) inner product on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            is required to be positive definite, as is that on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+spaces"} tangent spaces
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+spaces"} tangent spaces
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            is only required to be non-degenerate.
          %p
            The group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
            of an inner product space is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group+of+an+inner+product+space"} orthogonal group of an inner product space
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            (or more generally a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            is equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ↦
                    %mover
                      %mi r
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \mapsto \overline{r}
            = succeed ";" do
              %em conjugation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            one typically consider the involution by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
          %p
            Then a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+inner+product"}> Hermitian
            )
            %em inner product
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mtext -
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mtext -
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle\text{-},\, \text{-}\rangle
                  \\;\colon\;
                  V \times V \to k
          %p
            that is (1–3)
            %em sesquilinear
            (or
            %em bilinear
            when the involution is the identity) and (4)
            %em conjugate-symmetric
            (or
            %em symmetric
            when the involution is the identity). That is:
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 0, x \rangle = 0
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, 0 \rangle = 0
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x + y, z \rangle = \langle x, z \rangle + \langle y, z \rangle
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y + z \rangle = \langle x, y \rangle + \langle x, z \rangle
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi c
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle c x, y \rangle = \bar{c} \langle x, y \rangle
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, c y \rangle = \langle x, y \rangle c
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle = \overline{\langle y, x \rangle}
              \.
          %p
            Here we use the
            %em physicist's convention
            that the inner product is antilinear (= conjugate-linear) in the first variable rather than in the second, rather than the
            = succeed "," do
              %em mathematician's convention
          %p
            Note that we use the same ring as values of the inner product as for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalars"} scalars
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, c y \rangle = \langle x, y \rangle c
            is written with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            on the right for the case that we deal with noncommutative division ring.
          .query
            %p
              Are the two conventions really equivalent when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is noncommutative? —Toby
          %p
            (The axiom list above is rather redundant. First of all, (1) follows from (3) by setting
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = 0
          %p
            An
            %strong inner product space
            is simply a vector space equipped with an inner product.
          %p
            We define a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|{-}\|^2}\colon V \to k
            by
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} = \langle x, x \rangle
            %strong norm
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to be the square root of this expression in contexts where that makes sense, but for us
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|{-}\|^2}
            is an atomic symbol. The norm of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is
            %strong real
            in that it equals its own conjugate, by (4).
          %h2#definite Definiteness
          %p
            Notice that, by (1),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle 0, y \rangle = 0
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \;|\; \forall y,\; \langle x, y \rangle = 0 \}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspace"} linear subspace
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 0
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|0\|^2} = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \;|\; {\|x\|^2} = 0 \}
            may not be a subspace. These observations motivate some possible conditions on the inner product:
          %ul
            %li
              The inner product is
              %strong semidefinite
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x \;|\; {\|x\|^2} = 0 \}
              %em is
              closed under addition (and hence is a subspace); it's
              %strong indefinite
              if there are
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} = 0
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi y
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|y\|^2} = 0
              but
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mi y
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 2
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x + y\|^2} \ne 0
            %li
              The inner product is
              %strong nondegenerate
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle = 0
              for all
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              %strong degenerate
              if there is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 0
              but
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x, y \rangle = 0
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              The inner product is
              %strong definite
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
              whenever
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} = 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 0
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} = 0
              = succeed "." do
                %strong nondefinite
          %p
            (In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inequality+relation"} inequality relation
            relative to which the vector-space operations and the inner product are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+extensional+function"} strongly extensional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ne
            in them. We can also use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contrapositives"} contrapositives
            to put
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ne
            in the other conditions, which makes them stronger if the inequality relation is
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+relation"} tight
          %p
            An inner product is definite iff it's both semidefinite and nondegenerate. Semidefinite inner products behave very much like definite ones; you can mod out by the elements with norm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            to get a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+vector+space"} quotient space
            with a definite inner product. In a similar way, every inner product space has a nondegenerate quotient.
          %p
            Now suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            are standardly so equipped, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b - a
            is a nonnegative real.) Then we can consider other conditions on the inner product:
          %ul
            %li
              The inner product is
              = succeed "," do
                %strong positive semidefinite
              = succeed "," do
                %strong positive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} \geq 0
              always.
            %li
              The inner product is
              %strong positive definite
              if it is both positive and definite, in other words if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} \gt 0
              whenever
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 0
            %li
              The inner product is
              = succeed "," do
                %strong negative semidefinite
              = succeed "," do
                %strong negative
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} \leq 0
              always.
            %li
              The inner product is
              %strong negative definite
              if it is both negative and definite, in other words if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x\|^2} \lt 0
              whenever
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 0
          %p In this case, we have these theorems:
          %ul
            %li A positive or negative inner product really is semidefinite (as the terminology implies).
            %li
              Conversely, a semidefinite inner product is either positive or negative, at least if we use
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/xor"} xor
              negative, as long as it is nonzero in the sense that at least some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lang{x,y}\rang
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apart"} apart
              from zero. And of course, the zero inner product is the only one that is both positive and negative.)
            %li
              Hence, a definite inner product is either positive or negative definite. (We can strengthen this to ‘xor’ and state it constructively if we assume that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is nonzero in that there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              with
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \ne 0
            %li Conversely, an inner product is indefinite if and only if some norms are positive and some are negative. (No constructive caveats here!)
          %p
            Negative (semi)definite inner products behave very much like positive (semi)definite ones; you can turn one into the other by multiplying all inner products by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
          %p
            The study of positive definite inner product spaces (hence essentially of all semidefinite inner product spaces over partially ordered fields) is essentially the study of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field"} topological field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %a{:href => "http://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Krein_space"} English Wikipedia article
            on
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Krein space
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Krein+space"} ?
          %p
            All of this definiteness terminology may now be applied to an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \mapsto \langle{x, T y}\rangle
            is another inner product (on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dom T
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+operator"} positive operator
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"}> Euclidean space
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                (over
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            %li
              %p
                Finite-dimensional modules over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℍ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebras"} semisimple Lie algebras
                with the negative of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
                are positive-definite inner product spaces.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+abelian+groups"} inner product abelian groups
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+structure"} orthogonal structure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+object"} self-dual object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+of+vector+bundles"} inner product of vector bundles
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bra-ket"} bra-ket
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Original discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+inner+products"} Hermitian inner products
            in the context of defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            (as mathematical foundations of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li#vonNeumann30
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                , p. 64 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Allgemeine Eigenwerttheorie Hermitescher Funktionaloperatoren
                %strong 102
                (1930) 49–131 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01782338"}> doi:10.1007/BF01782338
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"}> John von Neumann
                \:
              %p
                p. 21 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematische Grundlagen der Quantenmechanik
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-96048-2
              %p
                pp. 38 in:
                %em Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Mechanics
                Princeton University Press (1955) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400889921"}> doi:10.1515/9781400889921
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_Foundations_of_Quantum_Mechanics"} Wikipedia entry
          %p
            Textbook accounts in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+V.+Kadison"}> Richard V. Kadison
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+R.+Ringrose"} John R. Ringrose
                %em Fundamentals of the theory of operator algebras
                Vol I
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elementary Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 15
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/gsm-15"} ISBN:978-0-8218-0819-1
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Blackadar"}> Bruce Blackadar
                , §I.1.1 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Operator Algebras – Theory of
                    = succeed "-Algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 122
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-28517-2"} doi:10.1007/3-540-28517-2
          %p
            Discussion of plain inner products (without star-involution) in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+objects"} self-dual objects
            in suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li#Selinger12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Selinger"}> Peter Selinger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Autonomous categories in which
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ≃
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \simeq A^\ast
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/SelingerSelfDual.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
